  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon, much of southwest Kansas was cooler and more \nhumid following last night's thunderstorm system. Cooler northeast \nwinds prevailed this afternoon, but these moist winds were become \nmore out of the east toward the Colorado line and on west toward the \nFront Range of Colorado where tonight's mesoscale convective system \n(MCS) will develop. Tonight's system will track farther south \ntonight vs. last night as cloud-bearing 300-500mb winds are more out \nof the northwest. This, combined with best low level moisture \ntransport vectors from the northern Texas Panhandle into far \nsoutheastern Colorado will really favor the Panhandles for tonight's \nheavy rainfall potential. Thus, much of our southwest Kansas region \nwill likely miss out out more rainfall with the exception of areas \ngenerally south of a Johnson to Meade line which is the only area we \nwill carry 60+ POPs tonight. \n\nAs we head into Monday and Tuesday, a Pacific jet streak will \nadvance across northern California and Nevada...eventually reaching \nthe Northern Rockies of Idaho, Montana, and northern Wyoming, \ninducing a leeside trough/surface low well to our north-northwest. \nAs a result, the focus for thunderstorm activity will shift back \nnorth into western Nebraska (Monday evening) and across eastern \nNebraska and northeastern Kansas Tuesday evening. As southwest \nKansas dries out (for those that received 1\"+ rain), we will warm \nback up such that by Tuesday, a return to upper 90s/near 100 degree \nhighs are likely. Given how much rain fell across our western \ncounties (along/west of Highway 83), Tuesday highs will probably \nfall a little short of 100 degrees, but the humidity could push heat \nindices above 100 instead. \n\nAs we head into Wednesday and beyond, another cold front will push \nsouth across Kansas, with perhaps even cooler air than today's \nfront. Much will depend, however, on how much of this cooler air \nmass is enhanced by remnant convective outflow from a potential large \nMCS rolling across the northeastern portion of Kansas. After that, \nglobal models and ensemble systems are showing a fairly sharp upper \nlevel ridge amplification centered across the Rockies with much of \nKansas in between the sharp ridge axis and a deep trough axis \nfarther east. This may make temperature forecast a little more \ndifficult for a few days Wednesday through roughly Saturday, \ndepending on if/how strong a backdoor cold front out of the \nnortheast moves into southwest Kansas. The latest ECMWF model shows \nthis ridge flattening with increased hot southwesterly downslope \nwinds from roughly Sunday into the Day 8-10 period, so it would not \nappear we would be done with 100-degree heat yet.",
